---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The original manual spent a third of its length on Google Docs mechanics: font
// sizes, line spacing, where the divider sits, which cell to leave empty, matching a
// table's formatting to an exemplar. The stylesheet decides all of that now and the
// editor offers no way to change it, so those sections are gone rather than rewritten.
const sections = [
  {
    id: 'voice', title: 'Voice',
    paras: [
      'Articles are written in the third person. First-person pronouns (I, we, us, our) do not appear in article text, and neither do second-person ones (you, your).',
      'The Talopedia has no opinions. Do not editorialise. A leader is not infamous, a policy is not controversial and a battle is not devastating unless the article attributes that description to someone named. Cut words that assume the reader agrees: clearly, obviously, naturally, of course, it is worth noting, interestingly. A fact that matters is shown to matter by the prose around it rather than announced.',
      'Collective nouns are singular: the cabinet has decided, not the cabinet have decided. The same goes for army, government, company and committee.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'naming', title: 'Naming and article titles',
    paras: [
      'A subject known by several names lists the notable equivalents at the very start, straight after the bolded name, with a transcription wherever the original script would be opaque:',
    ],
    example: '**Miyamoto Shogunate** (宮本幕府, Aldrige: *Miyamoto Bakufu*, *MEE-yah-moh-toh BAH-koo-foo*)',
    after: [
      'The title is the name most commonly used in Avium. Where two subjects share a title, disambiguate in parentheses with the broadest accurate category: Shimonosaki (city) and Shimonosaki (prefecture).',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'linking', title: 'Linking',
    paras: [
      'Link a subject the first time the body names it, and not again. The sidebar is a separate zone, so a subject linked in the body is still linked on its first appearance there.',
      'Link what a reader would plausibly want to follow. Not every proper noun needs one.',
      'Link out to Wikipedia only where the article there covers a direct real-world parallel, and do it sparingly.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'italics', title: 'Italics',
    paras: [
      'Italicise non-English words that have not entered ordinary English, and scientific names. Loanwords that have naturalised stay upright: samurai, vice versa, Gestapo, carte blanche, status quo. When in doubt, no italics.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'bold', title: 'Bold',
    paras: [
      'Bold belongs in three places: the subject where the article opens, the subject where a section opens, and the subject a bullet point introduces, with its alternative names. Never for emphasis mid-sentence.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'abbreviations', title: 'Abbreviations',
    paras: [
      'Write an abbreviation out in full the first time, with the short form in parentheses after it: the Shogunate Armed Forces (SAF). Afterwards the short form alone is fine. Body and sidebar are independent, so each expands on its own first use.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'dates', title: 'Dates and measurements',
    paras: [
      'Write years in full and anchor every date. Never write recently, currently, soon or now: they are wrong within a year and nobody comes back to fix them.',
      'If you use any system of measurement other than metric, give the metric equivalent alongside it.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'ooc', title: 'In character and out',
    paras: [
      'Articles are written from inside Avium, as a reference work produced there. Do not mention the setting as fiction, or refer to authors, episodes or narrative events as things outside it.',
      'Where an article carries information not publicly known in character, tick <b>Contains out-of-character information</b> in page settings. The notice is added for you; do not write it into the text.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'grammar', title: 'Grammar',
    paras: [
      'No contractions. A hyphen joins a compound modifier (six-year term); an en dash carries a range (1928–1930) and a pairing (the Nichirin–Guandong Treaty). Oxford commas are yours to choose, but choose once per article. Otherwise follow ordinary English.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'starting', title: 'Starting an article',
    paras: [
      'Pick the article type in page settings and the editor lays out the sidebar that type uses. Fill in the rows that apply and leave the rest; empty rows are never written to the file, so an unused field costs nothing.',
      'There are no font, size, colour or spacing controls anywhere, and there is nothing to match against another article. A section heading looks the way it does because it is a section heading.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'fallbacks', title: 'Anything not covered here',
    paras: [
      'Check Wikipedia’s Manual of Style. If that does not settle it either, ask a roleplay admin.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title="Manual of Style" description="How articles on the Talopedia are written.">
  <div class="article">
    <p class="lead">
      How articles are written. Everything the editor and the stylesheet already decide
      has been left out, so what remains is the part that is yours to get right.
    </p>

    {sections.map((s) => (
      <section class="mos">
        <h2 id={s.id}>{s.title}</h2>
        {s.paras.map((p) => <p set:html={p} />)}
        {s.example && <p class="mos-eg" set:html={s.example.replace(/\*\*(.+?)\*\*/g, '<b>$1</b>').replace(/\*(.+?)\*/g, '<i>$1</i>')} />}
        {s.after?.map((p) => <p set:html={p} />)}
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead { color: var(--muted); margin: 2px 0 20px; }
  .mos h2 { margin-top: 26px; }
  .mos-eg {
    padding: 9px 12px; border-left: 3px solid var(--rule);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--band) 45%, transparent);
  }
</style>
